{% comment %}
  Full view — 800x480 on an OG, 1040x780 on a TRMNL X. Six pair rows with
  sparklines, eight on the X. Merge variables come from the Worker's /rates
  response.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
    THE TRACKS
    Pair, rate and change are fixed widths, HI / LO sizes to its content, and
    TREND takes what is left (1fr). The reverse (a fixed sparkline with 1fr on
    the bounds) left ~119px of empty space inside the last cell, stranding the
    numbers at the far edge. The rate floor is the widest nine-character rate
    at this tier; the change track is the pill at its widest (`+1235%`), and
    is wider on the X, where the pill is `lg:label--large`.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    THE TYPE SCALE
    The rate is the number the panel exists to show, so it is the largest thing
    on it: `value--base` (38px), with the pair at `value--small` (26px) beside
    it, the two tiers a clear step apart so the row reads pair-then-rate rather
    than as one line of bold. The change and the bounds step down again, to
    `label`. The header is `label` too, bold, so it reads as a header rather
    than a caption.

    A TRMNL X keeps the same rate and pair. Only the pill steps up
    (`lg:label--large`): a larger rate took its width from the trace, and the
    X's extra 240px is better spent there, where it takes the trace from a
    quarter of the row to two fifths.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    PORTRAIT: TWO LINES PER ROW
    At 480px a one-line row has no room for a trace. So the tracks become
    pair, rate (1fr, right-aligned against the pill) and change; the trace
    spans the first two on a second line (`portrait:col--span-2`) with HI / LO
    beside it under the pill. The header follows the row: PAIR, LATEST and
    CHANGE over their columns, and beneath them, a tier smaller
    (`portrait:label--small`), the trend label at the trace's left edge and
    HI / LO over its figures. The second line is smaller so that it reads as
    the heading of the row's second line, not as two more columns.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    THE PLOT HEIGHT
    The trace fills the row: (panel - chrome) / rows - inset, in the custom
    properties below so each breakpoint can restate one term. --fx-chrome is
    the header, its rule and the title_bar, --fx-inset the row's padding plus
    its 1px rule, and in portrait also the first line and the gap under it,
    --fx-rows the rows that breakpoint shows. The terms are set so the last
    row ends 7 to 11px above the title bar on every device.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %} Rows a large screen shows: the plot height below divides by it. {% endcomment %}
{% assign fx_rows_lg = rows | size | at_most: 8 | at_least: 1 %}
<style>
  .fx--full .fx-row,
  .fx--full .fx-head { grid-template-columns: 140px minmax(208px, auto) 86px 1fr minmax(82px, auto); gap: 20px; }
  .screen--lg .fx--full .fx-row,
  .screen--lg .fx--full .fx-head { grid-template-columns: 140px minmax(208px, auto) 100px 1fr minmax(82px, auto); }
  .screen--portrait .fx--full .fx-row,
  .screen--portrait .fx--full .fx-head { grid-template-columns: 140px 1fr 86px; gap: 8px; }
  .screen--lg.screen--portrait .fx--full .fx-row,
  .screen--lg.screen--portrait .fx--full .fx-head { grid-template-columns: 140px 1fr 100px; }
  .fx--full { --fx-top-pad: 10px; --fx-chrome: 96px; --fx-inset: 21px; --fx-rows: 6; }
  .screen--lg .fx--full { --fx-rows: {{ fx_rows_lg }}; }
  .screen--portrait .fx--full { --fx-chrome: 110px; --fx-inset: 68px; }
  .fx--full .fx-dot  { r: 4.5px; }
  .fx--full .fx-spark svg { height: calc((var(--screen-h) - var(--fx-chrome) - var(--fx-top-pad)) / var(--fx-rows) - var(--fx-inset)); }
</style>

<div class="layout layout--col layout--justify-start">
  {% if fx_failed %}
    <div class="fx-error flex flex--col flex--center-y text--center">
      <span class="title">Rates unavailable</span>
      <span class="label label--gray 1bit:text--black">
        {{ error | default: "No data returned" }}
      </span>
    </div>
  {% else %}
    <div class="fx-list flex flex--col gap--none pt--2.5 fx--full">
      {% comment %}
        The window names the TREND column but governs three of them: the change
        figure, the sparkline and hi/lo are all measured over it. It sits here
        rather than in the title bar because a column header is read with the
        numbers, which is what keeps CHANGE from being taken for a daily move.
      {% endcomment %}
      <div class="fx-head grid pb--1">
        <div class="flex gap--none"><span class="label text--bold">PAIR</span></div><div class="flex flex--right gap--none"><span class="label text--bold">LATEST</span></div><div class="flex flex--right gap--none"><span class="label text--bold">CHANGE</span></div><div class="flex flex--center-x portrait:flex--left gap--none portrait:col--span-2"><span class="label text--bold portrait:label--small">{% if range_label %}{{ range_label }} {% endif %}TREND</span></div><div class="flex flex--right gap--none"><span class="label text--bold portrait:label--small">HI / LO</span></div>
      </div>

      {% comment %}
        EIGHT ROWS ON A LARGE SCREEN, SIX ELSEWHERE
        The field allows eight pairs. Six rows of 38px rate fill a TRMNL OG;
        the TRMNL X has 300px more height, so it shows all eight. Rows seven
        and eight are `hidden` and `lg:grid`, the framework's own breakpoint
        classes, so only a large screen displays them; the plot height above
        divides by the rows a large screen actually has, so six pairs on an X
        still fill it. Portrait X is also `lg` and taller still.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% for row in rows limit: 8 %}
        <div class="fx-row grid py--2.5 portrait:py--2{% if forloop.index > 6 %} hidden lg:grid{% endif %}">
          <div class="fx-pair value value--small text--bold">{{ row.from }}<span class="fx-arrow text--regular px--0.5">&rarr;</span>{{ row.to }}</div>
          <div class="fx-rate value value--base value--tnums text--bold text--right">{{ row.rate_str }}</div>
          <div class="fx-chg flex flex--right gap--none"><span class="label text--bold value--tnums px--2 py--1 lg:label--large {% if row.change_pct < 0 %}label--filled{% else %}label--outline{% endif %}">{{ row.change_str | replace: "-", "&minus;" }}%</span></div>
          {% assign fx_end = row.points | split: " " | last | split: "," %}
          {% assign fx_end_y = fx_end[1] | times: 100.0 | divided_by: 30 %}
          <div class="fx-spark flex gap--none portrait:col--span-2 portrait:pr--3">
            <svg class="fx-plot block" width="100%" height="30">
              <svg class="block" viewBox="0 0 200 30" width="100%" height="100%" preserveAspectRatio="none">
                <polyline points="{{ row.points }}" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/>
              </svg>
              <circle class="fx-dot" cx="100%" cy="{{ fx_end_y }}%"/>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div class="fx-range flex flex--col flex--between gap--none text--right"><span class="label value--tnums">{{ row.hi_str }}</span><span class="label value--tnums">{{ row.lo_str }}</span></div>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>

{% comment %}
  Full view: .title_bar comes AFTER .layout. TRMNL's
  `.view--full .layout:has(+.title_bar)` rule reserves the height and adds the
  gap only when the bar is the layout's next sibling.
{% endcomment %}
<div class="title_bar">
  {{ fx_icon }}
  <span class="title">Exchange Rates{% if env %} ({{ env | upcase }}){% endif %}</span>
  <span class="instance">
    {% if fx_failed %}No data{% else %}<span class="fx-dates text--default">{{ fx_dates }}</span> &middot; ECB{% endif %}
  </span>
</div>
